DNS :- Domain Name System
Simple example -
When you type: google.com -> your computer needs an IP address to communicate with Google's servers.
DNS answers: google.com -> 142.250.x.x
So DNS is essentially the phonebook of the Internet.

nslookup :- 
Simple example - 
when we type : nslookup example.com - 
Typical output:
Server:         192.168.1.1
Address:        192.168.1.1#53
Non-authoritative answer:
Name:   example.com
Address: 93.184.216.34
Reverse DNS lookup :-If you have an IP address and want to see whether it maps to a hostname:
nslookup 8.8.8.8
You might get:
Name:    dns.google
Address: 8.8.8.8

A — IPv4
nslookup -type=A example.com

AAAA — IPv6
nslookup -type=AAAA example.com

MX — Mail servers
nslookup -type=MX example.com

NS — Name servers
nslookup -type=NS example.com

whois — Domain Registration Information
Example :- Try - whois example.com
You might find:
Creation Date: ...
Registrar: ...
Name Server: ...
Domain Status: ...
This can help establish when a domain appeared and which registrar/DNS infrastructure it uses.

